  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 AM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nThe center of the upper ridge had shifted to the southeastern U.S. \nper the 07Z water vapor imagery while a shortwave trough lifted \nnortheast through the Pacific Northwest. The upper ridge was still \ninfluencing the weather over the forecast area even with a more \nsouthwesterly flow overhead. At the surface low pressure was observed\nover the high plains with a inverted trough axis extending through \nsouthern NEB and into northern IA. \n\nOverall there isn't a lot of change to the forecast that calls for \nhot and dry weather to persist through the weekend. The RAP and NAM \nshow some signal for elevated shower development around sunrise \nthis morning and again Thursday morning. Although the isentropic \nlift is progged to be somewhat limited in area. For now most models \nfail to generate any QPF this morning and the HRRR has isolated \nshowers Thursday morning. So have kept a dry forecast this morning \ndue to the lack of precip from the models and will hold off on \nadding a POP to Thursday to see is models are consistent in the \nlift. Aside from that there isn't much signal for organized forcing \nto generate precipitation as the consensus has the upper ridge \nredeveloping over the central plains beginning Friday and remaining \nover the area through Sunday. The NBM continues to under perform with \nhigh temps so have leaned more on adiabatic mixing of 925MB and \n850MB temps from the operational solutions to keep highs around 101 \ntoday. Decent southwesterly winds look to mix the boundary layer out \nagain today so heat indices are not expected to be that much hotter \nthan the actual air temp. However models prog persistent southerly \nlow level flow by late in the week which should allow better gulf \nmoisture to move in and increase low level humidity. So while mixing \nmay be a little more limited going into the weekend, higher levels \nof humidity may actually cause the heat index to increase. Don't \nhave any issues with keeping the heat advisory and if there are no \nbig changes in the later model runs, it may be prudent to extend it \nbeyond Friday. \n\nBy early next week, the GFS ensembles and ECMWF ensembles diverge on \nthe synoptic pattern with more members of the GFS wanting to flatten \nout the upper ridge while a majority of the ECMWF members try to \nmaintain the ridge. So there is lower predictability by the \nTuesday/Wednesday time frame. In either case the prospect for a \nstrong push of Canadian air appears to be unlikely and the NBM/WPC \nforecast of keeping temps in the middle 90s next week looks \nreasonable. There are some slight chance POPs in the forecast along \nthe NEB state line early next week, likely from the increasing \nspread in potential outcome and some of the ensembles flattening the \nridge. I don't have much confidence in precip chances until a more \nconsistent sign of the ridge breaking down becomes apparent.",
